---
// Real estate and property management cleaning — one of the three LEAF
// pages under the "Specialist cleaning" group in the Home Cleaning
// mega-menu (navigation.ts). Sibling of /airbnb-holiday-home-cleaning/ and
// /house-cleaning/ndis-cleaning/, built in the same pass as the first of those and sharing
// its structure deliberately.
//
// THIS PAGE HAS THREE INBOUND LINKS ALREADY WAITING FOR IT, which is
// unusual and shapes it:
//   - the homepage's persona card 3, "I manage properties"
//   - the footer and homepage service list, "Real estate and property
//     management"
//   - why-tlb.md §10, whose approved copy is "Agencies: give us the
//     difficult property. The one with the tight window or the awkward exit
//     condition. That is the job that tells you something."
// The last one is a promise made on another page, so this page picks that
// thread up rather than opening a new argument: it is the closing CTA below,
// verbatim.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS in content-plans/ for this page. As with
// /airbnb-holiday-home-cleaning/, it is not drafted from nothing — the Brand Foundation
// Document has a full audience section (§7 "Audience 3: real estate
// agencies and property managers", plus §3's fourth differentiator and §5's
// approved headline/CTA sets), and the agency relationship is the single
// most-repeated proof point on the whole site. Positioning traces to that
// document; page copy still needs sign-off.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH:
//   - INVOICING AND WHO PAYS. The BFD names "invoicing simplicity" as a
//     procurement criterion for this audience, and the page cannot say how
//     it works: does TLB invoice the agency, the owner, or the outgoing
//     tenant, and is it per job or consolidated monthly? A property manager
//     will not move a rent roll without knowing. Bracketed in §4 and
//     branded FAQ 4.
//   - CAPACITY. "A team with the capacity for your rent roll" is already
//     published on the homepage. How many vacancies a week can TLB actually
//     absorb, and what is the turnaround commitment? Bracketed in §4 and
//     branded FAQ 2.
//   - THE RE-CLEAN POLICY. What happens when an exit inspection fails.
//     Bracketed in §4 and branded FAQ 3, and it is the most important
//     bracket on the page — see the warning below.
//   - [insured] and [police-checked], inherited from every sibling. One
//     answer fixes the site.
//
// ⚠️ DO NOT FILL THE RE-CLEAN BRACKET WITH THE WORDS "BOND BACK GUARANTEE"
// unless TLB genuinely offers one and can honour it. No cleaner controls
// whether a bond is returned — that is the landlord's and the tribunal's
// call, and it turns on damage, rent and wear as well as cleanliness. A
// guarantee worded as though it covers the bond is an ACCC problem and it
// misleads the tenant, not the agency. A re-clean commitment ("we come back
// free if the exit inspection fails on cleaning") is honest, is what an
// agency actually wants, and is what the bracket asks for.
//
// NOT BUILT, deliberately: hero video, credibility signals, logo bar. The
// LogoBar component exists and an agency logo wall would be the strongest
// possible proof on this page — and it must not be built until TLB has
// written permission to display each mark. BFD §5.3 and §"Client and partner
// proof" both require it. See the reserved slot in the render below.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/sections/BeforeAfter.astro';
// Before/after pairs — real TLB job photos. The `sample-` files are flat
// illustrations and stay only where no real pair exists for that subject yet.
// The four data imports below sit here, between components, on purpose: Astro
// orders a page's inline CSS by import position, and they hold the slots the
// four before/after photo imports used to, so the built CSS order is unchanged.
import { comparisonColumns } from '../../data/comparison';
import { completeFootprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { localBusiness } from '../../data/business';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// The hero and cards 1 and 3 are this page's own, generated per
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES; the prompts are in src/assets/real_estate/IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
//
// Card 2 is REUSED from the end-of-lease page's folder, per IMAGE-GUIDELINES
// §5 ("reuse before generating; same subject = same file"). It is a property
// manager at a vacant handover with the keys on the benchtop, which is
// exactly this card's subject — generating a second version of that frame
// would make one idea look like two.
//
// ⚠️ NOT REUSED: src/assets/home/rental-property-cleaning-two-storey-house-exterior.jpg
// (and its duplicate service-real-estate-and-property-management.jpg), which
// is what the homepage runs for its "I manage properties" persona card. That
// photo is a North American suburban street — asphalt-shingle roof, board
// and batten gables, a sidewalk and front lawn to the kerb. It is not the
// Northern Rivers and it is not Australia. It predates the prompt sheets and
// has no prompt of its own. The homepage should be repointed at
// real-estate-cleaning-two-cleaners-arriving-at-a-rental-house.jpg below —
// see the sheet's opening note.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as every sibling.
// Before/after. Both pairs are REAL TLB job photography. See §7.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { theLineHeading, howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. ✅ REWRITTEN against the September 2026 copy-rewrite brief
// (workbook row 19, "For agencies and property managers").
//
// TITLE is the workbook's approved line verbatim. The old one split the mapped
// phrase `property management cleaning services` (150/mo) across "Real Estate
// Cleaners and", and at 67 characters it was being truncated in the result
// anyway. This one carries the phrase contiguously and fits, at 51.
//
// ⚠️ WORKBOOK CHANGE REQUEST, raised and not acted on: the approved title
// drops "real estate" entirely, and `real estate house cleaning` is worth
// 150/mo, exactly as much as the primary. Nine characters are spare. Something
// like "Property Management & Real Estate Cleaning | TLB Cleaning" would carry
// both. The approved line ships as approved; the eyebrow in §1 is what keeps
// "real estate" visible above the fold in the meantime.
//
// 🔴 DESCRIPTION IS *NOT* THE WORKBOOK'S APPROVED LINE. The approved meta reads:
//
//   "Cleaning for rent rolls, with nine employed cleaners, one contact and no
//    re-clean callbacks. Trusted by local agencies. Add us to your panel."
//
// The brief opens with a blocking change request against it, listing three
// separate problems in descending order of seriousness, and supplies the
// replacement used below. All three are taken:
//
//   1. "NO RE-CLEAN CALLBACKS" asserts an outcome, that re-cleans do not
//      happen, which is a performance claim sitting very close to the
//      guarantees BFD §12.2 rules out. §4's own bracket already knows the right
//      framing: "Word it as a re-clean, never as a bond back guarantee, no
//      cleaner controls whether a bond is returned." The meta had made a
//      stronger claim than the page is prepared to make. A re-clean commitment
//      is a promise about what TLB does; "no re-clean callbacks" is a promise
//      about what never happens, and TLB does not control the inspector.
//   2. "NINE EMPLOYED CLEANERS" is not in BFD §2.4's verified facts, and §2.4
//      opens "Use only what is confirmed." The comparable row in that table,
//      "Over 100 local homes, hosts and businesses", carries "Confirm the
//      number. Round down, never up." §2.4 also says in bold never to claim the
//      team is employed full-time; "nine employed cleaners" does not say
//      full-time, so it is not a direct breach, but it is one word away from
//      one and unverified either way. The same figure was live in the Airbnb
//      page's FAQ 4 and has come out of there too, on one ruling.
//   3. "TRUSTED BY LOCAL AGENCIES" is a softer phrasing of the unconfirmed
//      relationship BFD §2.4 lists as "Preferred supplier for real estate
//      agencies in the region", with the instruction to confirm whether these
//      are formal preferred-supplier arrangements or ongoing contracts and to
//      match the wording to the reality. Least serious of the three, still on
//      the list, and still live in the trust bar above.
//
// The replacement is the brief's own, verbatim, at 132 characters. It keeps the
// approved CTA as its final sentence, which is what §7's button label is taken
// from, and it keeps the headcount decision open rather than pre-empting it.
//
// ⚠️ WHAT THE OLD META GOT RIGHT, worth keeping if this is revisited: it named
// the three job types a property manager recognises, vacancy turnarounds, bond
// cleans and routine managed-property cleans, and it named the whole footprint
// including the Tweed. Its only real fault was that it ran 32 characters long.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// In `business`, on `areaServed`:
// BOTH STATES, like /airbnb-holiday-home-cleaning/ and unlike the commercial and NDIS
// pages. Deliberate: the BFD's audience section for this reader names
// agencies "across the Northern Rivers and Southern Gold Coast", and its
// differentiator section says TLB holds contracts in "the Northern Rivers
// and parts of the Southern Gold Coast". So the full `locationGroups` runs
// below rather than `nswLocationGroups`.
//
// ⚠️ "and parts of" is the BFD's own hedge on the Queensland side. The
// suburb list here does not repeat it. If the QLD coverage for agency work
// is genuinely partial, this page should say which parts.

// §1 Hero. ✅ H1 AND CTA REWRITTEN (copy-rewrite brief §4 and §7).
//
// This used to read: "The H1 is the BFD's own approved headline for this
// audience ('Stop chasing cleaners between tenancies.') and the CTA is its
// approved CTA from the same set ('Book a call with TLB')."
//
// The headline is NOT cut. It moves one line down into the new `subheading`
// slot, which is what that prop was added to Hero.astro for. "Stop chasing
// cleaners between tenancies" is the single best line for this reader on the
// site, because being chased is what BFD §7.3 names as the thing that ends a
// supplier relationship. The stray double space the live markup carried between
// "cleaners" and "between" is gone with the split.
//
// The new H1 is the page's actual argument. The exact mapped phrase is scored
// present or absent and was absent from all six places it could sit; BFD §7.3
// names capacity as one of the four things a property manager worries about,
// and §7.5's approved language is "the capacity to handle a rent roll rather
// than one job at a time". The H1 now says what the page spends 1,400 words
// proving.
//
// THE CTA CHANGE IS THE EASY ONE, unlike the Airbnb page's. "Add us to your
// panel" is the better of the two labels and it is what the searcher clicked:
// it names the actual transaction a property manager is being asked to make,
// which is not booking a clean but adding a trade to a list. This page's own
// non-branded FAQ is literally headed "What should an agency ask a cleaner
// before adding them to a preferred trades list?" So the workbook and the page
// agree here. §9.5 wants one primary CTA repeated, so all four move together.
//
// quoteCta.href is still the destination, so there is one form to build, not
// two. The lead leads with capacity, because that is the one claim a sole
// operator cannot match and the one the trust bar already makes.
//
// A SCENE PHOTO (family A): an empty, freshly finished rental with the keys
// on the bench. The emptiness is the point — it is a property between
// tenancies, which is the only moment this reader is buying. `imageFit` is
// unset and takes Placeholder's `cover` default; `ratio` is 1/1 because the
// source is 1024×1024, so a box matching the file crops nothing.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. A person who does not exist, in the
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES §3 canonical uniform, honest as generic service imagery
// and not presented as a named team member (§7). A real photo from a real
// handover would do more — and on this page it is unusually easy to get,
// since an empty property has no tenant to consent and nothing identifying
// in frame.
//
// In `hero`, on `kicker`:
// The eyebrow stays as it was, and now does a second job: with the H1
// carrying the mapped phrase, this is where "real estate" stays visible above
// the fold, which matters because `real estate house cleaning` is worth as
// much as the primary and the approved title drops it. See §0.
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Lands the mapped phrase contiguously inside the first sentence. The Oxford
// comma in "one point of contact, and vacancy cleans" also goes, per §9.2.

// §2 Definition. The answer-target block for the focus keyword and the
// first place the phrase appears in body copy. Paragraph two does the
// synonym work ("vacancy clean", "exit clean", "bond clean", "make-ready")
// so one page answers all of them.
//
// ⚠️ Paragraph two draws the bond-clean line at /house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning/ ON
// PURPOSE, rather than competing with it. That page is built, it is aimed at
// tenants AND agencies (its own §3 has cards for both), and its card 2
// already sends property managers here. Two pages both trying to own "bond
// clean" would split the term between them.

// §3 Pathway cards. The three jobs an agency hands out, in the order a
// property manager thinks about them: the urgent one, the one that gets
// measured, and the standing one.
//
// Destinations: the quote flow, the end-of-lease page (which owns the
// standard the inspection is measured against), and /why-tlb/. A leaf that
// links only to a form strands the property manager who is trialling.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on the description of "Standing work across the rent roll":
// ⚠️ Two brackets, inherited from the hub pages. One answer fixes
// every page they appear on.

// §4 What working with us looks like. THE MOST IMPORTANT BLOCK ON THIS PAGE.
//
// The BFD is unusually specific about how this audience buys: they procure
// through relationships, trial on a handful of jobs, and "value invoicing
// simplicity, communication and capacity above the lowest quote". So this
// block is deliberately NOT a cleaning task list — the task list lives on
// /house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning/. It is the operational answer to those three
// criteria, which is what actually wins a rent roll.
//
// ⚠️ THREE OF THE SIX CARRY BRACKETS, and they are the three a property
// manager will decide on. See the file header, including the warning about
// how the re-clean bracket must NOT be filled.
//
// In `workingWithUsItems`, on "Capacity, in writing":
// ⚠️ Real bracket. "Capacity for your rent roll" is already published
// on the homepage; the number behind it has never been stated.
// In `workingWithUsItems`, on "If it fails on cleaning, we come back":
// ⚠️ THE most important bracket on the page. Read the file header
// before filling it: do NOT word this as a bond guarantee.
// In `workingWithUsItems`, on "Invoicing that suits your trust accounting":
// ⚠️ Real bracket, and the quiet dealbreaker. The BFD names invoicing
// simplicity as a procurement criterion for this reader.

// §5 "Worth knowing" — the interruption block. Callout, tone="note".
//
// The lever for this reader is the consequence framing: what inaction costs
// an agency is not a dirty property, it is rework and reputational exposure.
//
// ✅ REWRITTEN (Brand Foundation v2.0 §3.2) — the heading was "The cheap
// vacancy clean is not cheap. It is a second inspection." and the body used
// "cheap" twice more. §3.2: "Never use the words cheap, budget, affordable,
// competitive rates or premium. Each signals in a direction we do not want.
// The position is certainty, not a price point."
//
// The argument is unchanged and is not about a competitor — it is about what
// a rejected clean costs the reader, which is §7.3's named worry ("A bond
// dispute... creates a tribunal matter, an unhappy tenant and an unhappy
// landlord"). Only the vocabulary changed, and the last line now makes the
// point through what TLB would rather be judged on.

// §6 What a rent roll actually needs. TextBlock dark — the long-form
// persuasion slot the sibling pages all carry.
//
// Written as what a property manager should ask, not as what TLB claims.
// That register is the commercial hub's, and it is the one that survives a
// reader who has been sold to before. Paragraph 3 is unchanged for exactly
// that reason: it invites the reader to ask US the same questions.
//
// ✅ REWRITTEN (Brand Foundation v2.0) — this section was titled "Why sole
// operators fail on a rent roll" and opened "Most cleaners who take agency
// work are one person with a car, and for a while that is fine — they are
// often excellent, and they are cheap."
//
// Three breaches:
//   1. §10.2 — "Never suggest an independent cleaner does worse work,
//      because the reader has probably had a good one." A section whose
//      heading is why they fail does exactly that, and in this market the
//      property manager reading it very likely uses one.
//   2. §3.2 — "Never use the words cheap, budget, affordable, competitive
//      rates or premium."
//   3. §9.4 — two paragraphs of running text about what somebody else
//      cannot do.
//
// The structural insight is genuinely useful and it survives: vacancies
// arrive in clusters, and capacity is what absorbs a cluster. §10.3 actively
// endorses that kind of published specific as how TLB wins. What changed is
// that the paragraphs are now about the shape of the work rather than about
// the shortcomings of the person currently doing it.

// §7 Before/after. Both pairs here are REAL TLB job photography.
//
// The pairs chosen here differ from the airbnb page's on purpose: kitchen
// and COOKTOP rather than kitchen and shower. The cooker is among the most
// commonly failed items on an exit inspection, so it is the pair that speaks
// to this reader. An oven-INTERIOR pair would speak to it harder still and
// is the next one worth shooting.
//
// These frames are REAL TLB JOB PHOTOGRAPHS, published with the client's
// permission and with nothing identifying in frame — no house numbers, street
// signs, mail, people, or names on doors. That is why this section no longer
// carries an "illustrative samples" note. Do NOT wire the `sample-` files back
// in without restoring a note that says so: a fabricated "before and after
// from a real job" is a false trust claim, not filler.
//
// THE PHOTO BRIEF: one fixed tripod position, nothing moved between frames.
// An oven and a shower on a real vacancy clean are the two easiest real
// pairs on this site to obtain, because a vacant property has no tenant in
// it and nothing identifying in frame.
//
// ⚠️ Consent conditions, from the end-of-lease page's brief and applying
// equally here: written permission from the agency AND from the outgoing
// tenant where the property is identifiable, and no identifying detail in
// frame — no house numbers, street signs, mail, people or names on doors.

// §8 What we do for agencies. The pills are the actual service lines an
// agency hands out, which doubles as internal linking into the cluster.
//
// ⚠️ Hrefs: the six that point at existing pages are real. The rest point at
// the quote flow rather than at a guessed slug, because unlike the
// commercial premises list there is no menu entry for them to match — a
// made-up slug here would be a 404 with nothing planned behind it.

// §10 Comparison table. 🔴 ROWS REBUILT for this page (copy-rewrite brief §18).
//
// The INTRO LINE is untouched and is the best framing line of any comparison
// table on the site: it converts the table from marketing into procurement.
// The table underneath was not delivering on it.
//
// What was here: `comparisonRows` from src/data/comparison.ts, the standard
// residential template, on a B2B page. The old note in this slot argued it was
// on-target because row 5 named the agency relationship. The brief disagrees,
// and it is right:
//   - the corner cell asked "Who cleans your home" of a property manager who
//     does not own any of the properties;
//   - one row was about holiday lets, which is somebody else's page;
//   - "Reminders" and "How you book" are consumer concerns, and BFD §7.6 names
//     that register explicitly as the language that fails with this reader.
//
// PAGE-LOCAL `rows`, not an edit to the shared file, and not
// `serviceComparisonRows()` either: this needs different rows, not the shared
// ones minus the holiday-let one. The homepage, /why-tlb/ and
// /commercial-cleaning/ still render the approved table in full, and the brief
// does not cover them — the same call the existing per-page variant block in
// src/data/comparison.ts already records.
//
// ✅ EVERY ROW IS DRAWN from something this page already says in prose or in a
// bracket, so no new claim is introduced. They are BFD §7.4's five things that
// move this buyer, plus the two §7.3 worries the prose already covers.
//
// ⚠️ ONE DELIBERATE DEPARTURE FROM THE BRIEF, in row 2. The brief's cell for
// the independent cleaner reads "Not possible". That is a verdict on a
// competitor, not a fact about a delivery model, and BFD §10.2 forbids it
// outright ("Never suggest an independent cleaner does worse work, because the
// reader has probably had a good one") — the same breach this page's §6 was
// already rewritten once to fix. "One property at a time" says the identical
// thing as a fact about how the work is organised, which is the standard §9.4
// sets: "'Whoever is rostered that day' is a fact. 'They will let you down' is
// not." Every other cell is the brief's, verbatim.
//
// ⚠️ ROW 5 IS CONDITIONAL. "A re-clean commitment in writing" is the framing
// §4's bracket asks for and the framing the §0 meta now uses, so the three are
// consistent. But the commitment's actual terms — the window it applies in,
// what it covers, whether it is free — are still unconfirmed. If that bracket
// comes back empty, DELETE THE ROW rather than softening it: a vaguer promise
// in a procurement table is worse than no promise.

// In `comparisonTable`, on `cornerLabel`:
// The corner cell keeps the sitewide label. "Who cleans your home" was the
// problem and it was a ROW label, not this one.

// §11 Branded FAQs — what property managers ask us. Three carry brackets;
// all three are launch-blocking (see the file header).
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "How quickly can you turn a vacancy around?":
// ⚠️ Bracketed. The first question a property manager asks.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "What happens if the exit inspection fails on cleaning?":
// ⚠️ Bracketed, and do not fill it with the words "bond back
// guarantee" — see the file header.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "How does invoicing work?":
// ⚠️ Bracketed, and the quiet dealbreaker for this reader.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Are your cleaners employed or subcontracted?":
// Approved claim: the comparison table's first row, and the BFD's
// headline differentiator for the business as a whole.

// §12 Non-branded FAQs — the long-tail block, aimed at what agencies and
// landlords search rather than what they ask us.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and FAQ 2 is the sensitive one. What a
// tenancy agreement can require of an outgoing tenant is set by residential
// tenancy law and differs between NSW and QLD, and it is contested — carpet
// cleaning and professional-cleaning clauses in particular. It is answered
// here at the level of "there are limits, and here is where to check" and
// points at the guide the nav already promises, rather than stating a rule.
// Do not "improve" it by adding a specific without a compliance read.
//
// The guide link (/guides/what-the-law-actually-says-end-of-lease-cleaning-in-nsw-and-qld/)
// is taken verbatim from navigation.ts, where it is listed and unbuilt. It
// will 404 until that guide ships.

// §13 Closing CTA. This is why-tlb.md §10's approved copy, picked up
// verbatim, because that page sends this reader here on exactly that
// promise. A page that is linked with a specific offer and then does not
// make it reads as a bait.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/house-cleaning/real-estate-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Real estate and property management"). This
// file owns the layout. The notes above explain why each section says what it
// says; they stay here because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would
// drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'house-cleaning/real-estate-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The text of the page's one primary button. It always links to the
    // site-wide quote page, so only the words are editable (see §1).
    buttonLabel: section.text,
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    // A card whose button is left empty gets the page's primary button.
    pathways: section.pathwayCards.extend({
      cards: z.array(section.pathwayCards.shape.cards.element.extend({ cta: section.optional(section.link) })).min(1),
    }),
    workingWithUs: section.iconGrid,
    worthKnowing: section.callout,
    rentRoll: section.textBlock,
    beforeAfter: section.beforeAfter,
    // A tag left without a link goes to the site-wide quote page (see §8).
    services: section.tagCloud,
    // The rows are this page's own (see §10); the columns are the shared ones.
    comparison: section.comparisonTable.omit({ columns: true }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading, like the towns, is the shared one for every full band.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ subheading: true }),
    bookingFaq: section.faq,
    generalFaq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/house-cleaning/real-estate-cleaning/',
};

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  // See the `areaServed` note above.
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers and Tweed NSW, and the Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

// quoteCta.href is the destination, so there is one form to build (see §1).
const pageCta = { label: page.buttonLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '1/1');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
  cta: card.cta ?? pageCta,
}));

const serviceTags = page.services.tags?.map((tag) => ({ ...tag, href: tag.href ?? quoteCta.href }));

---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? pageCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid across
        both sections. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026. Measured over this
        frame's own darkest region under the copy column, 75% gave 4.22:1 — an
        AA FAIL. 90% gives 5.96:1. Backdrop.astro's `opacity` doc carries the
        method. Same treatment as every sibling. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={page.pathways.heading} headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading={page.workingWithUs.heading}
      lead={page.workingWithUs.lead}
      items={page.workingWithUs.items}
      cta={page.workingWithUs.cta ?? pageCta}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={page.worthKnowing.heading} body={page.worthKnowing.body} cta={page.worthKnowing.cta} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.rentRoll.heading}
      body={page.rentRoll.body}
      cta={page.rentRoll.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.rentRoll.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* Drag the handle to wipe between the two frames of each pair. Both
        pairs are real TLB job photography — see the §7 comment for the
        consent conditions any replacement has to meet. */}
    <BeforeAfter
      heading={page.beforeAfter.heading}
      lead={page.beforeAfter.lead}
      pairs={page.beforeAfter.pairs}
      note={page.beforeAfter.note}
      beforeLabel={page.beforeAfter.beforeLabel}
      afterLabel={page.beforeAfter.afterLabel}
    />

    {/* ⚠️ RESERVED SLOT: agency logo wall (LogoBar.astro, variant="list").

        This is the strongest proof available to this page and to the site.
        The trust bar above already claims "Preferred supplier for real
        estate agencies in the region" in words; the marks would make it
        checkable, and BFD §"Client and partner proof" specifically notes
        that competitors display agency logos and TLB should seek permission
        to reference its agency relationships.

        NOT BUILT because permission is required per mark, at principal
        level, and none has been recorded. A logo wall assembled without it
        is both a trademark problem and the exact "fake proof"
        IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 rules out. Drop it in here, between the
        before/after and the service pills, once the permissions exist. */}

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.services.heading}
      subheading={page.services.subheading}
      tags={serviceTags}
      note={page.services.note}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={page.comparison.heading}
      cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
      lead={page.comparison.lead}
      columns={comparisonColumns}
      rows={page.comparison.rows}
      footnote={page.comparison.footnote}
      cta={page.comparison.cta}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    {/* ✅ THE FULL FOOTPRINT, per the copy-rewrite brief §20, which specifies
        this list town for town.

        The hero and the service pills above both promise the Tweed and this
        band used to drop it entirely, so the page stated two different
        footprints. On this page the omission is commercially specific: agencies
        with rent rolls in Tweed Heads, Banora Point and Murwillumbah are
        exactly the referral relationships BFD §7.1 says are worth dozens of
        individual bookings, and the page was telling them it does not work
        there. The header menu on this page already carried the wider list, so
        this was a template inconsistency rather than a writing problem. */}
    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={footprintSubheading}
      tags={page.whereWeClean.tags}
      groups={completeFootprintLocationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={theLineHeading.fast}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.condition, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* ✅ BOTH FAQ HEADINGS RENAMED (copy-rewrite brief §22 and §23).

        The page carries two FAQ blocks and the generic "Frequently Asked
        Questions" on the first made the split look like a build error. The
        first block is about working with TLB, the second is about the category.

        The second heading also lands `property management cleaning services`
        contiguously, which is the H2 keyword component the page previously half
        scored, and drops the Title Case that BFD §9.2 rules out. */}
    <Faq heading={page.bookingFaq.heading} lead={page.bookingFaq.lead} items={page.bookingFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.generalFaq.heading} lead={page.generalFaq.lead} items={page.generalFaq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? pageCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
